You write your code a file called the source code file
Now you create an executable by using the go build command.

go build -o <name-of-binary> source-code.go 

-o <name-of-binary> is optional 
If you don't provide the binary name is same as the source code file name

Then you can execute that using ./binary on say a Mac or Linux system

One interesting thing to note is that one can take that binary 
and just run it on any other system, as long as it has the same 
OS and the CPU architecture.
That system also don't need to have the Go runtime installed.

The go runtime is embedded in the binary, so the sie of the binary is large 
compared to gcc in C

=======

When you just however execute, 
go run main.go 
In this step too, the executable is created and is deleted once the binary completes. 
It is just hidden from us. 
you can see the folder in which this gets created by using the --work option 


